From: GS2107 <gs2107@local>
Subject: [PATCH] econet-eth: single TX queue, TX/RX path fixes and checksum/TSO offloads

- One TX/RX netdev queue per port. All packets go to the same hardware TX ring; 256 soft queues multiplied qdiscs (256 fq_codel instances) and BQL state without changing what reaches the hardware, and a queue could be stopped with none of its own packets in flight, so nothing woke it.
- Ring-full (-EBUSY, nothing queued) freed the skb and then returned NETDEV_TX_BUSY, so the stack requeued and later sent freed memory. Keep the skb and re-check the ring after stopping the queue to close the stop/wake race with the TX-done NAPI.
- BQL: account sent bytes under the ring lock before the doorbell; complete once per TX-done poll; reset the queue on open.
- TX-done: shared by the NAPI and the stop path; napi_consume_skb for sent packets; a record for an entry that is already free is skipped.
- Stop: let the hardware finish the TX rings, consume every completion record, free what was never sent and reset the rings, so the next open neither processes records for freed entries nor resends descriptors that point at freed buffers.
- Open: enable NAPI before RX/TX DMA, unmask the DONE interrupts and poll once. An RX interrupt taken between DMA enable and napi_enable masked its bit while the schedule was refused, so receive stalled for good with a full ring (seen after a reboot with traffic on the WAN port).
- Do not set skb->hash from the PPE entry index: with the PPE off it is the same for every packet, and the constant L4 hash steered every flow to a single RPS CPU.
- RX buffers keep NET_SKB_PAD of headroom and the QDMA writes frames 2 bytes in (RX_2B_OFFSET). Frames used to start at the buffer start, so behind the 18-byte MediaTek-tagged header the IP header was only 2-byte aligned and MIPS emulated every unaligned load in the exception handler (about 17 emulations per packet, 825 million in an hour of benchmarks); forwarding also had no room to push headers. With the offset the emulation count stays at 0 and routed 4-flow TCP in the deployed configuration went from 370-500 to 761-925 Mb/s on GS2107 (same bridge-hairpin bench, 4 runs each, before/after builds). The MTU is capped at what one RX buffer holds (1708), so the GDM never passes a frame that would spill into a second descriptor.
- 512 RX descriptors per chain (128 ran out under 1 Gb/s forwarding).
- TX: IPv4 TCP/UDP checksum offload, scatter-gather and TSO, done as the vendor qdma_bm_transmit_packet_tso does them: one descriptor per buffer with NLS on all but the last (every descriptor completes on its own, the skb hangs off the last), ICO/TCO/UCO in the TX message, the TSO bit (bit 28, misnamed SCO before) with the MSS in the TCP checksum field. The hardware only parses the frames correctly once the DMA parses the MediaTek tag (CDMA_VLAN_CTRL bit 0, vendor macEN7512STagEnable); without it the checksums came out wrong and a TSO frame stalled the TX DMA. At boot DSA attaches before the conduit opens and the bit is set in ndo_open; when the driver is reloaded and the switch bound afterwards, it is set from ndo_features_check on the first frame. Checksum/GSO features are dropped for every frame while the bit is not set or the port is no DSA conduit, and for anything but plain IPv4/IPv6; on stop the bit is cleared only after the TX DMA is stopped. On GS2107 a single TCP stream sent by the router went from about 300 (offloads switched off with ethtool) to 360-440 Mb/s; forwarded TCP stays at the 1 Gb/s link limit of the bench (fraglist GRO leaves as the original segments).
- RX checksum: received frames were all marked CHECKSUM_UNNECESSARY without any check, so frames with bad TCP/UDP/ICMP checksums were accepted by the router itself (and GRO could merge them into aggregates that are re-checksummed on the way out). The GDM checks checksums only when it parses the MediaTek tag (stag_en bit0, vendor GDMA1_VLAN_CHECK), and with that bit set every frame took about 7 ms round trip and TCP fell to a few Mb/s, every received frame carrying a CPU reason (crsn=30, ppe_entry=1 on the one decoded), which suggests a detour through the FE/PPE path the vendor sets up with hw_nat.ko. Received frames are now CHECKSUM_NONE and the stack verifies them.
- TX checksum offload and TSO also for IPv6 TCP (TCO without ICO, TSO6), verified on GS2107 the same way; there is no vendor precedent. IPv6 UDP is checksummed in software because a computed zero has to go out as 0xffff there, which the hardware was not verified to do; a non-TCP/UDP IPv4 checksum request falls back to software as well.
- TX doorbell rung once per batch (netdev_xmit_more()), whenever the ring cannot take another full skb, and when the last frame of a batch is dropped.
- ndo_open no longer calls en75_qdma_use() (mutex, napi_enable) under the register spinlock.
- NAPI completes with the work done, so napi_defer_hard_irqs and gro_flush_timeout (runtime module parameters) take effect; threaded_napi (default on, as before) selects the NAPI mode at load time.

